      "text": "To determine the number of different starting lineups possible, we need to select 3 more players from the remaining 10 players after accounting for Ace and Zeppo.\n\nLet's break down the steps:\n1. We have 12 players in total.\n2. We need to select 3 more players from the remaining 10 players.\n\nThe number of ways to choose 3 players from 10 is given by the binomial coefficient \\(\\binom{10}{3}\\).\n\nThe binomial coefficient \\(\\binom{n}{k}\\) is calculated using the formula:\n\\[\n\\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\n\\]\nwhere \\(n!\\) (n factorial) is the product of all positive integers up to \\(n\\).\n\nApplying this formula to our problem:\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{3} = \\frac{10!}{3!(10-3)!} = \\frac{10!}{3! \\cdot 7!}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify this by canceling out the \\(7!\\) in the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{3} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7!}{3! \\times 7!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{3!}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\(3!\\):\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\\[\n\\binom{10}{3} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{6}\n\\]\n\nNow, we perform the multiplication and division:\n\\[\n10 \\times 9 = 90\n\\]\n\\[\n90 \\times 8 = 720\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{720}{6} = 120\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different starting lineups possible is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{120}\n\\]",
